\title{Truth vs provability}
\date{2026-03-02T18:18:34Z}
\tag{discrete}
\tag{draft}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{The key is to switch our perspective: rather than focusing on whether propositions are \em{true}, we want to know whether they are \em{provable}, \ie whether it is possible to provide specific (computational) \em{evidence} which establishes that they must be true.  We are not content to know that something \em{has to be} true, but require direct evidence.}

\p{As a metaphorical example, imagine we want to know whether a certain student attended a certain class.  But we don't accept just a plain answer of "yes"; we require direct \em{evidence}.  Of course, we would have to decide what counted as direct evidence; perhaps, say, we would require a written statement from the instructor, who saw them in class with their own eyes and recorded their presence in the gradebook.  Whatever we decided, we would only accept that direct evidence, and not \em{circumstantial} evidence: for example, if someone saw the student enter the class and then leave the class again later, we would not accept that as evidence in place of an actual statement from the instructor, even if it seems like it would logically imply that the student must have been in class the whole time and the instructor must therefore have observed and recorded their presence.}
